 "result": "The repo is `tessel` (a Flask-like layout). The type round-tripping is done by a tagging serializer.\n\n**`src/tessel/json/tag.py`**\n- `TypedCookieSerializer` (line 219) holds the registered tags in `tags` and `order`.\n  - `register()` adds a tag.\n  - `tag()` wraps a value as it is dumped.\n  - `untag()` restores the original value on load.\n  - `dumps()` and `loads()` do the JSON encoding and decoding.\n- `TypeMarker` (line 60) is the base class for tags. Each subclass defines a `key`, plus `to_json()` and `to_python()`. The base `tag()` produces `{key: to_json(value)}`.\n- The concrete tags:\n  - `TagTuple` (key `\" t\"`) stores a tuple as a list.\n  - `TagBytes` (key `\" b\"`) stores bytes as a string, base64-encoded.\n  - `TagDateTime` (key `\" d\"`) stores a datetime as an HTTP date string.\n  - Others are `TagDict` (`\" di\"`), `TagMarkup`, `TagUUID`, plus pass-through `PassDict` and `PassList`.\n\n**`src/tessel/sessions.py`**\n- `session_json_serializer = TypedCookieSerializer()` (line 273) is the shared instance.\n- The cookie session interface class (around line 300) sets `serializer = session_json_serializer` and passes it as `serializer=self.serializer` (line 316) to the signing serializer, so the cookie payload goes through it.\n\nOn dump, the first tag whose check matches the value wraps it as a single-key dict, for example `{\" t\": [...]}`. On load, a single-key dict whose key matches a tag is passed to that tag's `to_python()`, which restores the tuple, bytes or datetime.\n\nI only looked at the class and method names via grep, so I haven't confirmed the exact encodings (base64, HTTP date) by reading the method bodies.",
